Z||000000||Daleford||01-04-2001||07:51 PM||||Transport Canada [RIV] has a problem with kitcars being imported into Canada. They won't let them in! My question is: When does a customized Fiero become a kitcar? Is a non-stretched Fiero sporting new bodypanels considered a kitcar? Is a stretched rebodied Fiero a kitcar?  Any help out there, because the RIV isn't!<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||01-04-2001||08:01 PM||||find a customs lawyer. you'll need one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maninblack||01-05-2001||09:49 AM||||baciclly, anything that is not "stock",engine<BR>suspensions,body panel etc (excluding radio<BR>equitment),would be consider a kit car in the<BR>eyes of Canadian Custom.Otherwise if the mods is done in side Canada, it would not be a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||01-05-2001||04:12 PM||||The countach in my sig was titled, registered and insured in PA as an '85 Fiero. When I sold it to a guy in Maryland, he had to go through all kinds of sh!t to register it. It seems like everywhere you go it's something different. Sorry, I don't know anything about Canadas regs.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
